Here **params means the latent update z**, not the full policy vector theta.
The full policy has D={{ full_parameter_dim }} parameters, while you only optimize d={{ rank }} latent coordinates (d << D).

The random-subspace mapping used by the optimizer is:

1. Sample G_t in R^(D x d), with each G_t[i,j] ~ Normal(0, 1).
2. Orthonormalize its columns: A_t = qr(G_t), so A_t^T A_t = I_d.
3. Decode your proposal z into the full policy:
   theta_(t+1) = clip(theta_t + alpha A_t z, -6, 6), where alpha={{ projection_scale }}.
4. Project a historical policy theta_i into the current latent coordinates:
   z_hat_i = clip(A_t^T (theta_i - theta_t) / alpha, -6, 6).

Thus f(params) below means f(theta_t + alpha A_t z). Historical z_hat values are
least-squares directional approximations in the current subspace. For every
neighborhood, params=0 is that neighborhood's anchor; all anchors use the same A_t.
Your returned z is always applied to the CURRENT policy theta_t.
{% else %}
params represent a linear policy. 
f(params) is the episodic reward of the policy.
{% endif %}
